# Sprint 4 — Student Portal

## Overview

Sprint 4 gives students their front door: viewing what teachers have posted, and submitting homework through the same marking engine teachers use. No marking logic is duplicated — homework submission reuses Sprint 3's `submissions.py` path directly, same pipeline as teacher-side marking.

## Goals

- Let a student view study materials and assignments
- Let a student submit homework for an assignment and see the marked result, using the existing engine
- Guarantee a student can never submit homework attributed to another student, even by a tampered request
- Let a student view their own marked results, and only their own
- Carry forward Sprint 3's interface and access-control standards rather than establishing new ones

## What was built

- **Study materials view** — list and detail view of `StudyMaterial` items, visible to all students for now (no class scoping yet — same known limitation carried from Sprint 3)
- **Assignments view** — list view of `Assignment` items (title, instructions, due date), each showing whether the logged-in student has already submitted homework for it
- **`Paper.assignment`** — new nullable FK linking a submission back to the assignment it answers, distinct from its memorandum
- **Homework submission** — student selects an assignment, uploads a photo, submits through Sprint 3's existing marking pipeline: same compression, same AI call, same defensive parsing
- **Server-set student ownership** — `Paper.student` is set from `request.user` server-side, never from any client-supplied field. Covered by an explicit test that attempts to submit with a spoofed student ID and confirms it's rejected/ignored.
- **Graceful failure state** — reuses Sprint 3's clear retry pattern on a failed marking attempt, no crash, no duplicated UI logic
- **Own results view** — a student can see their own marked papers (score, per-question feedback, most recent first); the same ownership boundary from Sprint 3 prevents viewing another student's results, enforced at the view level
- **Access control** — all views reject non-student roles, consistent with Sprints 1 and 3
- **Tests** — role rejection, the student-spoofing attempt, the mocked marking flow, and assignment-submission-status display

## Implementation notes

Decisions worth having on record.

- **`Paper.assignment`, a nullable `SET_NULL` FK.** It records which assignment a
  submission answers, distinct from the memorandum it is marked against. Nullable
  because a teacher marking a loose exam script is not answering any assignment;
  `SET_NULL` rather than `CASCADE` so deleting an assignment can never delete a
  learner's marked work. Declared with a string reference (`"classroom.Assignment"`)
  to avoid an import cycle, since `classroom` already points at `marking`.

- **The submission pipeline was reused, not rebuilt.** Homework goes through the
  same `marking/submissions.py` `submit_for_marking` as teacher marking; the only
  additions were an `assignment=` argument and a student-only form. Compression,
  storage, the AI call, and defensive parsing are all the Sprint 2/3 code.

- **Server-set attribution is structural, not a check.** The homework form
  (`HomeworkSubmissionForm`) has one field: the photo. The assignment comes from
  the URL and `Paper.student` from `request.user`. There is no student field to
  spoof — a tampered `student=` in the body is simply never read. A test posts
  that tampered request and asserts the paper still belongs to the logged-in
  student.

- **Ownership boundary mirrors Sprint 3.** A student's results are filtered by
  `student=request.user`; `my_result_detail` does `get_object_or_404(pk=pk,
  student=request.user)`, so another student's id returns 404 (the paper is
  excluded before the lookup) rather than 403 (which would confirm it exists).

- **One result presentation, two audiences.** The marked/failed result body was
  extracted into `marking/_result_body.html`, included by both the teacher's
  `paper_detail` and the student's `my_result_detail`. Each supplies its own
  navigation (retry URL, back link) via `{% url ... as %}`, so the marking
  result is rendered one way, and the failure/retry state a student sees is
  literally the teacher's, not a copy.

- **Per-sprint test grouping.** `run.sh sprint <n>` and `run.sh sprints` run the
  suite one sprint at a time with a banner and a pass/fail summary, so a
  regression names the sprint that owns it instead of hiding in a single total.
  The module-to-sprint mapping lives in `run.sh`; the per-sprint counts sum to
  the full-suite count, which is the check that every test belongs to exactly one
  sprint.

## Definition of done

- [x] Student can view study materials and assignments
- [x] Student can submit homework for an assignment and see the marked result
- [x] `Paper.student` is proven server-set, not client-controlled — covered by an explicit spoofing test
- [x] A failed marking attempt shows the same clear retry state as the teacher flow
- [x] Student can view their own results, and cannot access another student's results by URL-guessing
- [x] All views reject non-student roles at the view level
- [x] Tests cover role rejection, the spoofing attempt, the mocked marking flow, and submission-status display

## Interface standards carried forward

18px body text, 48px minimum tap targets, AA contrast, visible focus, works without JavaScript — the bar Sprint 3 established, not a one-off for that sprint alone.

## Out of scope for this sprint

Teacher visibility into homework results (Sprint 7's dashboard), facial recognition attendance (Sprint 5), parent notifications (Sprint 6), photo-to-transcribe (a teacher-authoring feature from Sprint 3, not relevant to students).

## Known limitations

- **No class/roster structure yet** (carried over from Sprint 3) — study materials and assignments are visible to all students, not scoped to a class.

- **Parent-student linking is still self-declared and unverified**
  (carried over from Sprint 1) — unchanged by this sprint.

## Next up — Sprint 5

Build facial recognition attendance: a teacher-triggered single scan per student, once at morning arrival and once at afternoon departure.